  slug: "senior-living-certificate-of-need-senior-living",
  title: "Certificate of Need for Senior Living Projects | Apex Grid",
  description: "CON usually targets nursing beds, not assisted living — but in CON states it shapes strategy, timing, and competitive risk.",
  h1: "Do senior living projects need a certificate of need?",
  topic: "Senior Living Engineering",
  answer: "Certificate of need is a state health-planning regime: in the roughly two-thirds of states that retain it, new nursing beds, major capital expenditures, and certain services need state approval demonstrating community need. Assisted living is generally outside CON — which is one reason the sector has grown faster than nursing. The strategic implications remain: in CON states, acquiring or relocating existing nursing beds can be the only path to a skilled component, competitors can challenge applications, and timelines stretch by a year or more. Florida repealed nursing-home CON in 2019; Texas, California, and Arizona never had it — a meaningful entitlement advantage for developers in those states.",
  directAnswer: "Usually not for assisted living — certificate-of-need programs in the states that have them typically apply to nursing facility beds and other high-cost services, not residential board-and-care. But in CON states, the nursing component of a CCRC or the competitive landscape around bed supply still shapes project strategy, and the rules change — confirm current status with the state health planning agency before site control.",
  sections: [
    { heading: "What CON covers — and what it doesn't", body: "CON programs typically cover nursing facility beds, hospitals, and big-ticket services; thresholds vary by state. Assisted living, independent living, and memory care are usually exempt — but 'usually' is doing work in that sentence, because states define categories differently and a project that looks like assisted living but functions like nursing can draw scrutiny. Read the state's definitions against the actual program, not the marketing brochure." },
    { heading: "Strategy in CON states", body: "In CON states, the nursing-bed supply is effectively capped, which makes existing beds valuable and new-bed applications competitive events. Developers either acquire bed rights, partner with existing operators, or design the project to stay cleanly outside CON jurisdiction. The application itself is a documented proceeding — need methodology, public comment, competitor opposition — and the engineering package should be far enough along to survive scrutiny but not so far that a denial wastes design fees." },
    { heading: "States without CON", body: "In non-CON states the entitlement path is shorter and the competitive moat is execution rather than regulatory permission — which cuts both ways, since competitors face the same low barrier. Speed to market, site control, and operational excellence decide outcomes. The engineering implication is pace: design teams in non-CON states should be built for fast entitlements support, because the window to lead a submarket is measured in months." }
  ],
  faqs: [
    { question: "Which states have certificate of need?", answer: "Roughly two-thirds of states retain some CON program, with wide variation in what is covered. Nursing facility beds are the most commonly regulated category. Confirm current status with the state's health planning agency — legislatures revisit these laws regularly." },
    { question: "Does CON apply to memory care?", answer: "Generally no, as memory care is typically licensed within residential categories outside CON. If the memory care unit is part of a nursing facility, the facility-level CON posture governs." },
    { question: "How long does a CON application take?", answer: "Commonly a year or more including application, review, public process, and potential appeals or competitor challenges. Build it into the project timeline before counting on a delivery date." }
  ],
